    {
      "filename": "The Police Camp Cottage. (52350398502).jpg",
      "title": "File:The Police Camp Cottage. (52350398502).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/The_Police_Camp_Cottage._%2852350398502%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Police_Camp_Cottage._(52350398502).jpg",
      "width": 2400,
      "height": 1800,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Bernard Spragg. NZ from Christchurch, New Zealand",
      "description": "The Police Camp Cottage at Butlers Green would have to be the most photographed structure in Arrowtown. The front door of this iconic building has long been locked, but it will soon be opened up again.\nThe cottage was built in 1863 and is Arrowtown's oldest surviving timber building. It was constructed from pit-sawn red beech and had hand-cut shakes on the roof. It was originally in Cardigan Street and was moved to its present site in 1991.\nThe Arrowtown Promotion and Business Association (that's us 😊) and the Lakes District Museum have worked with the community to restore the hut for use as an interpretation site. So from Saturday you'll be able to enter the building between 9am and 5pm. Inside you'll find lots of information about the Mahu Whenua open space behind our village and the flora and fauna there.\n\nThe official opening of the hut will be at 10am on Saturday 9 November. Public are welcome to attend the ceremony and there will be refreshments for invited guests following formalities.",
